{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.ZMod.Basic"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.norm3_ne_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.norm3_ne_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L139-L156","doc":"**Structural exclusion (mod-3 obstruction).** The value `−1` is not\nrepresented by the disc-12 norm form at all — by any lattice point, not merely\nby unit powers: reducing `a² − 3b² = −1` mod `3` demands `a² ≡ 2 (mod 3)`, and\n`2` is not a square mod `3`. This is the negative-Pell insolvability for\n`D = 3`, the cited invariant behind `pell3_norm`'s \"no parity flip\": the norm\n(Kummer/Galois norm of `ℚ(√3)/ℚ`) simply never takes the value `−1` on the\ncusp-torus order, while on the golden order it does (`goldenPow_norm_alternates`,\n`normPhi (goldenPow 3) = −1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.goldenPow_norm_alternates","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.goldenPow_norm_alternates","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L134-L137","doc":"The parity flip is genuine: the golden norm takes both signs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.norm3_pell3_eq_one_and_normPhi_goldenPow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.norm3_pell3_eq_one_and_normPhi_goldenPow","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L125-L132","doc":"**The structural contrast, in one statement.** On the cusp-torus order the\nnorm of every unit power is `+1`; on Bring's golden order it is `(−1)ⁿ`. Hence a\nsign that alternates with the winding number is available on the disc-5 order and\nstructurally unavailable on the disc-12 one — which is exactly why the sign flip\nmust live on Bring's surface rather than on the cusp torus. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.goldenPow_norm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.goldenPow_norm","kind":"theorem","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L114-L123","doc":"**The parity flip on Bring's golden order.** `N(φⁿ) = (−1)ⁿ`: the norm of the\n`n`-th power alternates with the parity of `n`. Contrast `pell3_norm`, where the\nnorm is constantly `+1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.normPhi_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.normPhi_step","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L107-L112","doc":"**The golden step NEGATES the norm.** This single `ring` identity is the\nwhole parity mechanism: multiplying by `φ` (whose norm is `−1`) flips the sign\nof `a² + ab − b²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.normPhi","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.normPhi","kind":"def","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L103-L105","doc":"The norm form of `ℤ[φ]` in the basis `(1, φ)`:\n`N(a + bφ) = (a + bφ)(a + bφ̄) = a² + ab − b²`, of discriminant `5`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.goldenPow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.goldenPow","kind":"def","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L96-L101","doc":"Powers of the fundamental unit `φ`, in the basis `(1, φ)`: `(a, b)`\nrepresents `a + bφ`, and `φ² = φ + 1` gives `(a + bφ)·φ = b + (a + b)φ`, i.e.\n`(a, b) ↦ (b, a + b)` — the Fibonacci recursion. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.pell3_norm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.pell3_norm","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L83-L92","doc":"**No parity flip on the cusp torus.** Every power of `2 + √3` has norm `+1`,\nso the disc-12 order cannot produce a sign that alternates with the winding\nnumber. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.norm3_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.norm3_step","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L77-L81","doc":"**The silver step PRESERVES the norm.** Multiplying by the fundamental unit\n`2 + √3` (which has norm `+1`) leaves `a² − 3b²` unchanged. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.norm3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.norm3","kind":"def","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L74-L75","doc":"The norm form of `ℤ[√3]`: `N(a + b√3) = a² − 3b²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.pell3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.pell3","kind":"def","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L67-L72","doc":"Powers of the fundamental unit `2 + √3`, in the basis `(1, √3)`:\n`(a, b)` represents `a + b√3`, and multiplying by `2 + √3` sends\n`(a, b) ↦ (2a + 3b, a + 2b)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.winding_lorentz_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WindingLatticeNormForm.winding_lorentz_form","kind":"theorem","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WindingLatticeNormForm.lean#L50-L61","doc":"**The winding Lorentz form (disc 12).** For the geodesic `u = c·(m + ℓω)`\nwith cusp shape `ω = (1 + s)/2` where `s * s = 3` (i.e. `s` stands for `√3`,\n`ω = e^{iπ/3}`), the doubled real and imaginary parts are `c(2m+ℓ)` and `c·ℓ·s`,\nand the indefinite pairing `Re² − Im²` collapses onto the integer form\n`(2m+ℓ)² − 3ℓ²` — the norm form of `ℤ[√3]`.\n\nStated over a generic `CommRing` with `s` given only by `s * s = 3`, so it holds\nfor any base ring carrying a square root of `3`; no ordering is used. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:winding-lattice-norm-form","label":"prop:winding-lattice-norm-form","md":"quantum-observable-universe/braids-and-knots/winding-lattice-norm-form.md"}],"signatureChunk":0}]}